# Terraform and Azure CLI Documentation

# What is Terraform?

Terraform is an Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tool developed by HashiCorp.

Terraform is used to:
- automate cloud infrastructure
- create Azure resources using code
- manage infrastructure consistently

Instead of manually creating resources in Azure Portal, we write code in `.tf` files.

Example:

```hcl
resource "azurerm_resource_group" "example" {
  name     = "example-resources"
  location = "Central India"
}

Terraform reads this code and creates the resource in Azure.

What is Azure CLI?

Azure CLI is a command-line tool used to interact with Microsoft Azure.

Azure CLI is used for:

Azure authentication
resource management
automation
scripting

Terraform uses Azure CLI authentication to connect with Azure.

Command:

az login

This command authenticates the user with Azure.

Terraform Installation
Step 1 — Download Terraform

Download Terraform from:

https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/install

Download:

windows_amd64 version for Intel/AMD systems
Step 2 — Extract Terraform

After extracting the ZIP file:

terraform.exe

will be available.

Step 3 — Create Terraform Folder

Create folder:

C:\terraform

Move:

terraform.exe

inside:

C:\terraform

Final path:

C:\terraform\terraform.exe
PATH Environment Variable

PATH is a Windows environment variable.

PATH contains folders where Windows searches for commands.

When we run:

terraform

Windows searches all folders inside PATH for:

terraform.exe

If Terraform folder is not added to PATH:

terraform is not recognized

error appears.

Add Terraform to PATH

Add this path into Environment Variables:

C:\terraform

Purpose:

allows Terraform command to work globally
Verify Terraform Installation

Command:

terraform -version

Expected output:

Terraform v1.15.2
Azure CLI Installation

Download Azure CLI:

https://aka.ms/installazurecliwindows

Install normally.

Verify Azure CLI

Command:

az version

Expected output:

{
  "azure-cli": "2.xx.x"
}
Azure Authentication

Command:

az login

What happens internally:

Browser opens
User logs into Azure account
Azure generates access token
Azure CLI stores token locally
Terraform uses this token automatically

Authentication flow:

User → Azure CLI → Azure Token → Terraform → Azure APIs
Terraform Project Structure

Example:

Azure-RG/
│
├── main.tf
├── terraform.tfstate
├── .terraform/
└── .terraform.lock.hcl
Terraform Configuration File

File:

main.tf

Code:

terraform {
  required_providers {
    azurerm = {
      source  = "hashicorp/azurerm"
      version = "4.71.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "azurerm" {
  features {}
}

resource "azurerm_resource_group" "example" {
  name     = "example-resources"
  location = "Central India"
}
Explanation of Terraform Code
terraform block

Defines required providers.

required_providers

Specifies Azure provider plugin:

azurerm
provider block

Connects Terraform with Azure.

Example:

provider "azurerm" {
  features {}
}
resource block

Defines Azure resource.

Example:

resource "azurerm_resource_group" "example"
Resource Name
example-resources

Actual Azure Resource Group name.

Location
Central India

Azure region where resource will be created.

Terraform Lifecycle
1. terraform init

Command:

terraform init

Purpose:

initializes Terraform
downloads Azure provider plugin
creates .terraform folder
2. terraform validate

Command:

terraform validate

Purpose:

validates Terraform syntax
3. terraform plan

Command:

terraform plan

Purpose:

previews infrastructure changes
shows resources Terraform will create

No resources are created yet.

4. terraform apply

Command:

terraform apply

Purpose:

creates infrastructure in Azure

Terraform:

reads .tf files
authenticates using Azure CLI token
calls Azure APIs
Azure creates resources
5. terraform destroy

Command:

terraform destroy

Purpose:

deletes Terraform-managed infrastructure
Important Terraform Files
File/Folder	Purpose
main.tf	Infrastructure code
.terraform/	Provider plugins
terraform.tfstate	Tracks infrastructure
.terraform.lock.hcl	Provider version lock
Terraform State File

Terraform creates:

terraform.tfstate

Purpose:

stores infrastructure information
tracks created resources
helps Terraform detect changes

Without state file:

Terraform cannot track infrastructure properly
